505 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Pwning the Domain: Credentialess/Username)
/E (Pwning the Domain: Credentialess/Username)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
506 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (The attacker sends out fake ARP answers by using a spoofing tool like Arpspoof or bettercap. It is conducted by opening two terminal in kali and send the command like so :)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
507 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (arpspoof -i wlan0 -t 10.0.0.1 10.0.0.62 # targeting router)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
508 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 209 0 R
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
509 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (arpspoof -i wlan0 -t 10.0.0.62. 10.0.0.1 # targeting victim)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
510 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 209 0 R
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
511 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (The falsified answers claim that the attacker's MAC address is the correct MAC address for both IP addresses, which belong to the workstation and router. By doing this, the workstation and router are tricked into connecting to the attacker's computer rather than to one another.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
512 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (From so on, rather of speaking with each other directly, the two devices communicate with the attacker by updating their entries in the ARP cache.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
513 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Now, the attacker is surreptitiously interfering with every conversation. we can check it via ARP table again :)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
514 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 209 0 R
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
515 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (So , If the data is not being encrypted or the protocol is not using SSL/TLS and authentication attempt is made it can provide the access to sensitive data or NTLM hashes , which can latter be crack to)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [11]
>>
